---
description:

Enforcement: Cursor (AI) must implement all items below automatically, and all pull requests (PRs) must pass the CI gates described before code is merged to main. Any failure prevents merge.

1. High-level mandate

1.1. The code must be production-grade, readable, maintainable, and secure.
1.2. Never introduce “useless” code: no dead code, no unused imports/variables, no commented-out code in main branches, no leftover debug scaffolding.
1.3. Every change must include tests (unit and/or integration) that prove the change works and that regressions are covered.
1.4. All PRs must update relevant documentation and changelog entries.

2. Tooling & automatic checks (required in every workspace/CI)

2.1. Formatting: Run flutter format (or dart format) on all changed files. CI rejects merges if files are not formatted.
2.2. Static analysis & lints: Run flutter analyze using flutter_lints (or an agreed linter config). Treat all warnings as failures for PRs. Configure analysis_options.yaml so that unused_import, unused_local_variable, dead_code, and avoid_print are treated as errors.
2.3. Code style: Use conventional commits style for commit messages (e.g., feat:, fix:, chore:). CI checks commit messages and rejects non-conforming ones.
2.4. Build generation check: Run flutter pub run build_runner build --delete-conflicting-outputs (or equivalent) in CI; any generated-code mismatch must fail the build.
2.5. Dependency audit: Run dart pub outdated and a dependency vulnerability scan (e.g., snyk or GitHub Dependabot) as part of CI; blocking severity vulnerabilities must be resolved before production releases.
2.6. Secrets scan: Run secret scanning tool on commits (no API keys, secrets, or credentials in code). Any secret found blocks the PR.
2.7. Test suite: Run flutter test (unit + widget tests) and any integration tests in CI. PR must reach configured coverage thresholds (see §6).
2.8. Performance & regression checks: Run lightweight performance checks (time to cold start, memory usage basic check) for major changes. Flag regressions >10%.

3. Pre-commit & pre-push local hooks (developer machine)

3.1. Install pre-commit hooks that run the following on changed files: dart format, flutter analyze (limited scope), flutter test (affected tests), and build_runner if generated files are changed.
3.2. The hooks must refuse commits that include: TODOs marked // TODO: remove or // FIXME in production code, commented-out large code blocks, or debug print() statements.
3.3. The hooks must check for accidental large binary files (>5MB) and block them; large assets must be added via the asset pipeline only.

4. Pull Request requirements & gating

4.1. All changes must be made on a feature branch; PR target must be develop or main depending on your branching policy.
4.2. Each PR must include: (a) description of the change, (b) linked issue or ticket, (c) list of tests added/updated, (d) screenshots or short demo GIF/video for UI changes, (e) impact analysis on notifications/scheduling logic.
4.3. PR must pass all CI checks listed in §2.
4.4. PR must have at least one human reviewer and at least one automated code-review tool (linter, static analyzer) approval. For changes touching critical modules (scheduling engine, notifications, encryption/backup), require two human reviewers including at least one senior engineer or code owner.
4.5. For security-sensitive changes (storage encryption, backup, Keychain/Keystore logic), require sign-off by a designated security owner.
4.6. Merges must be fast-forward where possible; do not merge with unresolved conflicts or “work-in-progress” markers.

5. Tests & coverage requirements

5.1. Unit tests: For all new or modified domain logic (e.g., ScheduleService, RRULE parsing, sequence handling), provide thorough unit tests covering edge cases (DST, timezone changes, BYDAY rules, COUNT/UNTIL).
5.2. Integration tests: For notification scheduling/rescheduling and persistence, provide integration tests that simulate scheduling, device restart (re-scheduling), and timezone changes. Use CI test harnesses and mocking as needed.
5.3. E2E tests (for release merges): Simulate the full user flow: create check, schedule, receive notification, perform Done, confirm analytics state changes. Provide at least one automated E2E scenario for core flow.
5.4. Coverage thresholds: Minimum 80% unit coverage on domain logic modules (ScheduleService, StorageService, NotificationService). Overall project coverage should be at least 70%. If thresholds cannot be met, the PR must include a written justification and a plan to address missing tests.
5.5. Flaky tests: Any test failing intermittently must be marked flaky and fixed before merging significant changes. CI must track flaky tests and show historic failure rates.

6. No “useless code” rules (static enforcement)

6.1. Unused code: CI must fail on any unused_import, unused_local_variable, dead_code, or unreachable code flagged by analyser. These are treated as errors, not warnings.
6.2. Commented blocks: Long commented-out code (>3 lines) in production files is forbidden. If a feature is experimental, place it behind a feature flag and keep code active, or move experimental code to a clearly named experimental branch.
6.3. No duplicated logic: If code is duplicated, the reviewer must request refactor to share logic or extract helpers. Use duplication detection tools and maintain a duplication ratio below agreed threshold (e.g., <3% duplication in core modules).
6.4. No dead feature flags: If feature flags are used, they must be documented and removed or folded within 3 sprints.

7. Quality & architectural rules

7.1. Separation of concerns: UI, domain logic, and services must be separated. The ScheduleService must not contain UI code.
7.2. Immutable models & null-safety: Use Dart null-safety and prefer immutable models (use final, data classes). Avoid dynamic.
7.3. Generated code policy: Generated files (from build_runner) must live in lib/src/gen or lib/gen and be clearly named; do not edit generated files manually — document regeneration steps in README. CI enforces generated files are current.
7.4. Complexity limits: Enforce cyclomatic complexity <= 12 for functions in domain/services; functions above this must be refactored. Use static tools to measure complexity.
7.5. Explicit error handling: Avoid catching broad exceptions. Any catch must either rethrow or wrap into a domain-specific error with logging. No silent failures.
7.6. API of services: Service classes must expose small, well-documented public APIs. Methods must have doc comments describing inputs, outputs, side effects, and exceptions.

8. Security & privacy rules

8.1. Secrets & keys: No keys or secrets in code. Use platform Keychain/Keystore or environment variables. CI must scan for secrets in commits.
8.2. Encryption: If encryption is used for Hive or backup, keys must be persisted in secure storage only and never logged or exfiltrated. Unit tests for encryption must validate correct behavior.
8.3. Backup import/export: All export files must be password-protected AES-encrypted; import must validate password and integrity. Tests must assert that wrong passwords fail gracefully (no partial restore).
8.4. Data minimization: Only store the minimal metadata required. Telemetry is opt-in only. If telemetry is added, offer a privacy toggle and collect only what is necessary, and log exact schema with opt-in confirmation.

9. Release discipline & changelog

9.1. Semantic versioning: Adopt SemVer. Tag releases with vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH. CI should create changelog entries automatically from commit messages (Conventional Commits).
9.2. Changelog: Each release must include a CHANGELOG.md entry describing user-visible changes, migration steps, and any breaking changes.
9.3. Release checklist: Before publishing an app release, verify: updated privacy policy linked, screenshots updated, test suite green, security scan clean, and confirm no TODOs or FIXMEs remain.

10. PR code review checklist (for reviewers)

For each PR, reviewers must confirm:
10.1. Linting & formatting pass.
10.2. Tests added and passing; coverage thresholds met.
10.3. No unused/dead/commented code.
10.4. PR description explains purpose and links issue.
10.5. Public APIs are documented; function/class docstrings present.
10.6. Any platform changes (Android manifest, iOS entitlements) are reviewed for permissions and privacy impacts.
10.7. Security: no secrets, correct key handling, proper encryption when required.
10.8. UX: small demo video or screenshots for UI changes provided.
10.9. Performance: no obvious regressions; large allocations or slow operations are avoided.
10.10. Accessibility: semantic labels present and voiceover friendly.
If any item fails, the reviewer must request changes and block merge.

11. Change management & modifications policy

11.1. Every change updates tests & docs: If you modify domain logic (scheduling rules, recurrence behavior, snooze logic), you must update SCHEDULE_SPEC.md and add/modify tests that prove the change. PRs missing spec updates are blocked.
11.2. Major behavior changes require migration plan: Changes to the schedule model or storage schema must include a migration plan and migration code. Tests must validate migration works from previous stored data.
11.3. Code owners & mandatory approvals: Files under lib/services/* and lib/models/* are owned by code owners; any change must be approved by owner. Add CODEOWNERS to repo.
11.4. Deprecation policy: Deprecated functions/classes must be annotated and kept for one major version with migration notes; remove only after next major version and document the removal.

12. Documentation & deliverables

12.1. Every PR must update README if new developer commands are added.
12.2. Maintain ARCHITECTURE.md, SCHEDULE_SPEC.md, LEGAL_CHECKLIST.md, DESIGN.md (or links to design assets). PRs affecting those areas must update the docs.
12.3. Provide inline doc comments for all public functions and classes. Use dartdoc style.
12.4. Provide a "how to test notifications on device" section in README for QA.

13. CI enforcement policy (summary)

CI must run the full pipeline on each PR: format -> analyze -> build-regenerate -> unit tests -> integration tests -> security scan -> coverage check -> artifact build.

Any failure stops the merge and posts clear failure reasons.

If builds succeed, PR may be merged after required human approvals.

14. Exceptions & escalation

14.1. If a PR cannot meet a rule (e.g., temporary lowered coverage for an urgent fix), the PR must include a written exception request with justification and remediation timeline. An authorized project lead must approve the exception and add a ticket to fix within one sprint.
14.2. For security exceptions, require explicit security owner approval and immediate mitigation plan.

15. Continuous improvement

15.1. Every sprint retro includes a brief review of lint/data from CI (false positives, flaky tests, and analysis rules) and may propose adjustments to analysis_options.yaml or CI.
15.2. Keep this Cursor Rule as a living document — any approved changes must be recorded in CONTRIBUTING.md.

16. Operational commands for Cursor (to be automated)

When Cursor receives a change request or generates code, it must execute this script sequence automatically (local & CI):

dart format .

flutter pub get

flutter pub run build_runner build --delete-conflicting-outputs

flutter analyze --no-fatal-infos (treat warnings as errors in CI config)

flutter test --coverage

Run coverage threshold checker (fail if below configured thresholds)

Run dependency audit script (e.g., dart pub outdated and security scanner)

Run secret scan (tooling such as git-secrets or GitHub actions)

Produce artifact and attach logs & coverage report to PR.

17. Final: acceptance for Cursor output

Cursor must only mark a feature as complete if:

All required tests pass and coverage thresholds are met.

All CI checks in §2 pass.

Documentation & changelog updated.

Human reviewers sign-off as required (§4).

No TODOs/FIXMEs remain in production files.
Only then should the code be merged to main and included in any release.
globs:
alwaysApply: true
